## BLUF

The gateway's audit middleware records `audit_logs.ip_address` from the **first** comma-separated `X-Forwarded-For` entry, which is the entry the caller writes, and it never reads `req.ip`. Everywhere else in the gateway the client address is `req.ip`, which with `trust proxy` = 1 is the **last** entry, the one the proxy appends. So one request carries two different "client addresses" inside the gateway, and the one stored in the audit row is the caller's choice. **This is a code read, not a measurement. Whether the live edge lets a caller-written first entry through to the row is UNMEASURED.**

## The code (READ at develop `4ccdc3b3e7cf`, 2026-11-06; nothing was run against it)

* `services/api-gateway/src/middleware/audit.ts:190-198`:

```
function getClientIp(req: Request): string | null {
  const xff = req.headers['x-forwarded-for'];
  if (typeof xff === 'string' && xff.length > 0) {
    return xff.split(',')[0].trim();
  }
  if (Array.isArray(xff) && xff.length > 0) {
    return xff[0];
  }
  return req.socket?.remoteAddress || null;
}
```

* The only call site is `audit.ts:280`; the value goes into `audit_logs.ip_address` (INSERT at `audit.ts:315`, value bound at `:326`). The file header (`audit.ts:17`) states the choice, "ipAddress from X-Forwarded-For (first hop) or req.socket.remoteAddress", and gives no reason.
* `services/api-gateway/src/index.ts:113` sets `app.set('trust proxy', 1)`. Every other reader of the client address uses `req.ip`: `index.ts:476`, `middleware/csrf.ts` (237, 378, 402, 422, 452), `middleware/errorHandler.ts:78`, `middleware/security.ts` (54, the login lockout key at 230 as `req.ip || 'unknown'`, 293), and every `express-rate-limit` instance through its default key. With `trust proxy` = 1, `req.ip` is the last entry.
* No test file under `services/api-gateway` references `getClientIp` (0 hits outside `audit.ts`), so nothing pins the first-entry read as deliberate.
* Edge configs, READ from the repo and not run: `docker/nginx-gateway/nginx.conf:133`, `nginx-production.conf:296` and `nginx-demo.conf:334` all set `proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for`, which appends nginx's `$remote_addr` to what the client sent. On that reading a caller-written first entry reaches the gateway unchanged. `nginx-demo.conf:100-107` adds `real_ip_header X-Forwarded-For` for a list of trusted source addresses; on my reading that changes what nginx takes as `$remote_addr`, not the leftmost text.

## Why it matters

An audit row's address is evidence of who did what, from where. If the first entry is the caller's to write, an actor can put any address into their own audit rows, and the row looks like every other. The single call site means the same function supplies the address for every row this middleware writes. Medium rather than High because the row also carries actor, path and time, and because whether a caller-written entry survives the edge has not been measured.

## Not measured (stated, not assumed)

* Whether the front door on Kintsugi, demo or production passes a caller-written first entry through to the gateway. The nginx reading above comes from the repo's config files, not from a running edge. Caddy sits in front on demo (`deployment/caddy/Caddyfile`) and I did not read its header handling.
* What value actually lands in `audit_logs.ip_address` for a request sent with a labelled first entry.
* A way to measure it with no box write: send one request through the real front door with a labelled `X-Forwarded-For` first entry (a documentation-range address such as `203.0.113.77`), then read the newest `audit_logs` row's `ip_address` read-only. That belongs to a box-read seat; the filing seat did not do it.

## Not decided here (a design choice, not made)

Reading the same address as `req.ip` would make the row agree with the limiter and lockout keys. The first-entry read may have been chosen on purpose, to record the original client behind a proxy chain; the header comment says what it does and gives no reason. Which one the platform wants is a ruling, and this ticket assumes neither.

## Related, not duplicates

KS 1492 (the per-IP limiters key on the last entry) and KS 618 (the client address is invisible on demo). Neither mentions `audit.ts` or the audit row; both were read in full.

Searched before filing (Linear, includeArchived, title and description): `getClientIp` 0 hits, `leftmost` 0, `x-forwarded-for` 3 (KS 618, KS 1492, KS 1524), `first hop` 3, `audit.ts` 10, `ip_address` 3, `XFF` 2. The five plausible candidates (KS 618, KS 1492, KS 236, KS 746, KS 1610) were read in full; none covers this.

Found by Seat C 35th while answering KS 1425. The proxy-addr 2.0.7 to 2.0.8 change does not touch this path in either version, because this function never calls proxy-addr. Filed on Wednesday's ruling of 2026-11-06; nothing was built.
